think Lindy-first crux on the journey: CAUM6 archive keys by position (arrival order); a keeper next needs to address by CONTENT — do I already hold this exact recording, can I fetch it by name; content-addressing is the tree's Tablecloth spine, read for years, underpinning provenance and the parts-marketplace sprite store
think wire.rye already computes the Sha256 over every payload (digest_len 32) — the content address is already carried; the decisive move is to let the desk key by it
think a new sibling module, not a change to archive.rye — CAUM6's selftest deposits the same frame 16x to prove the entry bound, which a de-duplicating store would collapse to one; changing it in place breaks that proof and accrete-never-break; grow catalog.rye beside archive.rye (log + catalog, both wanted)
think one-clock: TZ=America/New_York reads 02:47 but git committer clock and the whole CAUM0–CAUM6 stream stamp UTC ~06:4x; an NY stamp would sort before CAUM6 and break monotonicity, so follow the recent-stream UTC convention (064900) — later stays larger
obs deposit deframes verify-before-trust FIRST, computes address over the PROVEN payload; an address already held returns unchanged spending no bound (idempotent); a new address appends within entry-count and byte bounds refusing CatalogFull by name before any write; byte sum checked in u64 so it cannot wrap; copy is a plain bounded loop, never a bare memcpy
obs seven selftest blocks: same-bytes-one-address (kept once, count+1), distinct-bytes-distinct-address (both fetch byte-for-byte, manifest in order), tamper refuses DigestMismatch unchanged, entry bound refuses CatalogFull, byte budget refuses CatalogFull, a miss returns null not a guess, clear forgets whole
obs custody gate #2 untouched — no disk, no network, no key signs, no funds; the content address is an integrity identity the desk recomputes, not a signature; a signed catalog is a later gated rung
loom witness=caum_catalog_witness green=1 rungs=7 max_entries=16 max_bytes=32768 digest_len=32
